490 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 197 0 R
/T (A university campus contains many destinations connected by walkways, roads, entrances, stairs, and junctions. Students may need to reach classrooms and laboratories quickly, while visitors may need help finding administrative offices or an auditorium. Knowing the name of a building does not automatically reveal a practical walking route from the current position.)
/E (A university campus contains many destinations connected by walkways, roads, entrances, stairs, and junctions. Students may need to reach classrooms and laboratories quickly, while visitors may need help finding administrative offices or an auditorium. Knowing the name of a building does not automatically reveal a practical walking route from the current position.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
491 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 197 0 R
/T (Campus navigation is a useful Foundations of AI problem because it connects a clear goal with a representation of the environment and a sequence of actions. The goal is to reach a selected destination. The environment is a network of known campus locations and permitted walking connections. The actions are movements along those connections. A search procedure selects a route that satisfies the goal and the stated constraints [1], [2].)
/E (Campus navigation is a useful Foundations of AI problem because it connects a clear goal with a representation of the environment and a sequence of actions. The goal is to reach a selected destination. The environment is a network of known campus locations and permitted walking connections. The actions are movements along those connections. A search procedure selects a route that satisfies the goal and the stated constraints [1], [2].)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
492 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 197 0 R
/T (The proposed system treats buildings and junctions as graph nodes. Walkable connections are edges, each with a nonnegative distance. A route is a sequence of adjacent nodes, and its distance is the sum of the selected edge weights. A disconnected pair of locations must produce a clear failure message instead of an invented route.)
/E (The proposed system treats buildings and junctions as graph nodes. Walkable connections are edges, each with a nonnegative distance. A route is a sequence of adjacent nodes, and its distance is the sum of the selected edge weights. A disconnected pair of locations must produce a clear failure message instead of an invented route.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
