/T (Dijkstra search selects the frontier entry with the smallest accumulated path cost. On a finite graph with nonnegative weights, it returns a minimum-cost route when the goal is selected for expansion. It does not use a goal-directed estimate [2]. In the reference implementation, this mode is produced by setting the heuristic to zero.)
/E (Dijkstra search selects the frontier entry with the smallest accumulated path cost. On a finite graph with nonnegative weights, it returns a minimum-cost route when the goal is selected for expansion. It does not use a goal-directed estimate [2]. In the reference implementation, this mode is produced by setting the heuristic to zero.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
563 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 204 0 R
/T (A* assigns a priority f\(n\) = g\(n\) + h\(n\). The value g\(n\) is the best known distance from the start to n, while h\(n\) estimates the remaining distance to the goal. The heuristic can direct the search toward promising locations without changing the distance objective when its required conditions are satisfied [3].)
/E (A* assigns a priority f\(n\) = g\(n\) + h\(n\). The value g\(n\) is the best known distance from the start to n, while h\(n\) estimates the remaining distance to the goal. The heuristic can direct the search toward promising locations without changing the distance objective when its required conditions are satisfied [3].)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
