2996 0 obj
<<
/Title (A university campus contains many destinations connected by walkways, roads, entrances, stairs, and junctions. Students may need to reach classrooms and laboratories quickly, while visitors may need help finding administrative offices or an auditorium. Knowing the name of a building does not automatically reveal a practical walking route from the current position.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2978 0 R
/Next 3020 0 R
>>
endobj
2997 0 obj
<<
/Title (The proposed system treats buildings and junctions as graph nodes. Walkable connections are edges, each with a nonnegative distance. A route is a sequence of adjacent nodes, and its distance is the sum of the selected edge weights. A disconnected pair of locations must produce a clear failure message instead of an invented route.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2978 0 R
/Prev 3020 0 R
>>
endobj
2998 0 obj
<<
/Title (A route finder can reduce the effort required to interpret a static campus map. It can also explain why a familiar shortcut is unavailable when a path is closed or contains stairs. These features are particularly useful for first-time users and for users who request step-free travel.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2979 0 R
/Next 2999 0 R
>>
endobj
2999 0 obj
<<
/Title (The project focuses on a transparent search-based design. A user can inspect the selected path, distance, and constraint message rather than receiving an unexplained suggestion. This makes the system easier to demonstrate, test, and improve in an academic setting.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2979 0 R
/Prev 2998 0 R
>>
endobj
3000 0 obj
<<
/Title (This report covers the problem definition, campus graph design, navigation agent, destination lookup, route-search algorithm, implementation modules, illustrative interface, and reproducible evaluation. Its technical core is a JavaScript reference implementation of A* with a binary heap, together with a Dijkstra comparison obtained by disabling the heuristic.)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2980 0 R
/Next 3021 0 R
>>
