<<
/Title (Usability requires visible labels, keyboard-friendly controls, readable contrast, and error messages that tell the user how to proceed. The input form should not erase the current selection after an invalid query. Route descriptions should remain available as text even when the map cannot be viewed.)
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3124 0 R
/Prev 3146 0 R
/Next 3147 0 R
>>
endobj
3166 0 obj
<<
/Title (A weighted graph matters because the fewest connections need not form the shortest walking route. A three-edge route may be shorter than a two-edge route if its individual walkways are short. Breadth-first search is suitable for minimizing hop count in an unweighted graph; it is not generally suitable for minimizing the varied distances used here [2].)
/Dest [26 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3127 0 R
/Prev 3150 0 R
/Next 3151 0 R
>>
endobj
3167 0 obj
<<
/Title (A* assigns a priority f\(n\) = g\(n\) + h\(n\). The value g\(n\) is the best known distance from the start to n, while h\(n\) estimates the remaining distance to the goal. The heuristic can direct the search toward promising locations without changing the distance objective when its required conditions are satisfied [3].)
/Dest [27 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3128 0 R
/Prev 3152 0 R
/Next 3168 0 R
>>
endobj
3168 0 obj
<<
/Title (Both methods relax outgoing edges: if reaching a neighbor through the current node gives a smaller distance, the stored best cost and predecessor are updated. A priority queue chooses the next entry. Old queue entries must be ignored when a later entry contains a better cost for the same node.)
/Dest [27 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3128 0 R
/Prev 3167 0 R
/Next 3153 0 R
>>
endobj
3169 0 obj
<<
/Title (The reference implementation uses the Euclidean estimate h\(n\) = sqrt\(\(x\(n\)-x\(goal\)\)^2 + \(y\(n\)-y\(goal\)\)^2\). It measures straight-line distance in the same planar metre units as the sample edge weights. The estimate cannot include an arbitrary multiplier that would overstate the remaining distance.)
/Dest [27 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3153 0 R
/Next 3183 0 R
>>
endobj
3170 0 obj
<<
/Title (Filtering out stair links or closed links preserves that inequality for the remaining edges. If future costs include discounts, mixed units, or coordinates with an unsuitable projection, the assumption must be checked again. Setting h = 0 is a safe fallback when no justified spatial heuristic is available.)
/Dest [27 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3153 0 R
/Prev 3183 0 R
>>
endobj
3171 0 obj
<<
/Title (A browser interface can render campus nodes, edges, selected routes, and user controls. The Canvas API supplies drawing primitives for such a map, while normal HTML controls can provide the location selection and textual result panel [4]. The renderer should take the planner's output as data rather than calculate an independent route. JavaScript classes and modules provide a way to separate the map model, search algorithm, lookup function, and presentation code [5], [6]. This separation allows a route to be tested directly with known inputs. It also makes it easier to update a map label without modifying the priority queue.)
/Dest [28 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3154 0 R
/Next 3184 0 R
>>
endobj
