<<
/Title (For every sample edge \(u, v\), its weight is at least the straight-line distance between u and v. The triangle inequality then gives h\(u\) <= w\(u,v\) + h\(v\). This consistency property permits the graph-search implementation to finalize an expanded node without reopening it [3].)
/Dest [27 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3153 0 R
/Prev 3169 0 R
/Next 3170 0 R
>>
endobj
3184 0 obj
<<
/Title (A useful explanation identifies the objective and constraints: for example, "Shortest distance among step-free open walkways." The route list and edge-distance sum support that statement. A* is not a language model and cannot infer that a walkway is open merely from the name of a building.)
/Dest [28 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3154 0 R
/Prev 3171 0 R
/Next 3172 0 R
>>
endobj
3185 0 obj
<<
/Title (Printed maps and signboards provide location information but depend on the user to combine it into a route. A shortest-hop search treats every connection equally. A weighted route finder addresses varied walking distances. A heuristic search can also reduce the number of explored locations on suitable maps.)
/Dest [28 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3172 0 R
/Next 3198 0 R
>>
endobj
3186 0 obj
<<
