/Title (For each neighbor, the planner calculates candidate = g\(current\) + edgeWeight. When this is smaller than the neighbor's recorded best value, the distance and predecessor are updated and a fresh heap entry is added. Reaching the goal triggers path reconstruction through the predecessor chain.)
/Dest [32 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3155 0 R
/Prev 3175 0 R
/Next 3176 0 R
>>
endobj
3193 0 obj
<<
/Title (The closed-set rule is valid here because the Euclidean heuristic is consistent with every sample edge distance. The program checks that each edge is at least as long as its straight-line separation. A different cost model requires a new check or the h = 0 fallback.)
/Dest [32 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3176 0 R
/Next 3194 0 R
>>
endobj
3194 0 obj
<<
/Title (With a consistent heuristic and a binary heap, each permitted adjacency edge is considered when its source is expanded. Lazy duplicate entries can occupy O\(V + E\) space; queue operations give O\(\(V + E\) log\(V + E\)\) time. On this simple graph, E <= V^2, so the familiar O\(\(V + E\) log V\) bound also applies.)
/Dest [32 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3176 0 R
/Prev 3193 0 R
/First 3208 0 R
/Last 3208 0 R
/Count -1
>>
endobj
3195 0 obj
<<
